# Answer 1 · What Is Feminism? Radical versus Socialist Feminism (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-7

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one defines feminism and its common ground; part two compares the two schools point by point and ends with what they share.
@ask ① WHAT IS feminism?
@ask ② IN WHAT WAY is RADICAL different from SOCIALIST feminism?
(1) Introduction: feminism means equal rights plus action to win them; both schools belong to the 1960s–70s; they differ on the root cause
(2) What feminism is: an idea that women's disadvantage is man-made, and a movement to change it
(3) Common ground: women are disadvantaged; it is social, not natural; the personal is political
(4) Radical feminism: patriarchy is the deepest root; Millett, Firestone and Hanisch; transform family, body and culture
(5) Socialist feminism: capitalism and patriarchy work together; Mitchell and Hartmann; change economy and family
(6) The differences: root cause, unit of analysis, view of men, methods, key concepts and campaigns
(7) What they share: deep change, not reform; the family as a site of oppression
(8) Pakistan: radical insight behind laws on violence; socialist insight behind home-based and health workers' rights
(9) Conclusion: they are partners more than rivals — violence needs one, poverty the other
:::

## Decoding the question
The question has **two parts**. **"What is feminism?"** asks for a definition — a simple one of your own, a quoted one, and a short account of what all feminists share. **"In what way is … different"** asks for a **comparison**: the examiner wants **points of difference**, set side by side, not two separate essays. The best answers also note **what the two schools share**, because a difference only means something against a common background.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Feminism** is the belief that women and men should have **equal rights, dignity and chances**, together with **organised action** to achieve this.

#### ii) The two schools
**Radical** and **socialist feminism** both grew in the late **1960s and 1970s**, the "**second wave**". Both want **deep change** rather than mere legal reform.

#### iii) The argument
They differ on **the root cause**. For radical feminists the root is **patriarchy alone**; for socialist feminists it is **patriarchy and capitalism working together**. From this one difference, all the others follow.

### 2. What feminism is

#### i) A theory and a practice
Feminism is both an **idea** and a **movement**. As an idea, it says that women as a group are disadvantaged and that this disadvantage is **made by society**, not fixed by nature. As a movement, it organises to change that situation — through writing, protest, law and new ways of living.

#### ii) Two definitions
**bell hooks** (==2000==) called feminism "a movement to end **sexism**, sexist exploitation, and oppression" — its target is sexism, not men. The South Asian scholars **Kamla Bhasin and Nighat Said Khan** (==1986==) defined it as "an awareness of women's oppression and exploitation in society, at work and within the family, and **conscious action by women and men** to change this situation".

#### iii) Roots
The word came from **France** (Hubertine Auclert, ==1882==), but the ideas are older and not only Western: **Tarabai Shinde** (==1882==) and **Begum Rokeya** (==1905==) argued for women's dignity in South Asia.

### 3. What all feminists share

#### i) Five common claims
All schools accept that: (1) women are **disadvantaged**; (2) this is **social, not natural**; (3) the **personal is political**; (4) women's **experience is valid knowledge**; and (5) change is **possible**.

#### ii) Where they part
They disagree on the **cause** of oppression, **where** it happens, and the **remedy**. Liberal feminists blame **unequal laws** and want **reform**; Marxists blame **capitalism**; psychoanalytic feminists look to **early childhood**. The radical and socialist schools are the two great "**transformation**" schools of the second wave, and their quarrel is about the **root**.

### 4. Radical feminism

#### i) Meaning
"Radical" comes from the Latin ***radix***, **root**. Radical feminists hold that **patriarchy** — male power over women — is the **oldest and deepest** form of oppression, found in every society and every class.

#### ii) Thinkers
**Kate Millett** (*Sexual Politics*, ==1970==) showed that relations between the sexes are **power relations**. **Shulamith Firestone** (*The Dialectic of Sex*, ==1970==) traced oppression to **reproduction** and called women a "**sex class**". **Carol Hanisch**'s essay "**The Personal Is Political**" (==1970==) defended **consciousness-raising** groups.

#### iii) Where and how
Its main sites are the **family, sexuality, the body and violence**. Its remedy is to **transform** culture and family at the root — above all, to end men's control of women's bodies.

### 5. Socialist feminism

#### i) Meaning
Socialist feminism holds that women are oppressed by **two linked systems**: **capitalism**, which exploits workers, and **patriarchy**, which gives men power over women. **Zillah Eisenstein** (==1979==) called the combined system "**capitalist patriarchy**".

#### ii) Thinkers
**Juliet Mitchell** (*Woman's Estate*, ==1971==) named four structures: production, reproduction, sexuality and the socialisation of children. **Heidi Hartmann** ("The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism", ==1979==) called Marxism "**sex-blind**" and proposed **dual-systems theory**.

#### iii) Where and how
Its sites are **paid work and the family together**. Its remedy is to change both: **equal pay, public childcare, shared housework** and workers' rights.

### 6. The differences

::: fig compare | Radical and socialist feminism at a glance.
## Radical feminism
- Root: patriarchy alone
- Women as a "sex class"
- Men as a group benefit
- Consciousness-raising, women-only spaces
- Shelters, rape law, bodily autonomy
## Socialist feminism
- Root: capitalism and patriarchy together
- Women divided by class, united by sex
- Working-class men also exploited
- Alliances with workers' movements
- Equal pay, childcare, housework
:::

#### i) The root cause
Radical: **patriarchy came first** and would survive the end of capitalism. Socialist: ending capitalism **alone** would not free women, but neither would ending patriarchy alone — **both** systems must change.

#### ii) The unit of analysis
Radical feminism studies **sex**: women form one class. Socialist feminism studies **sex and class** together: a factory owner's wife and a factory worker's wife share some oppression but not all.

#### iii) The view of men
Radicals see **men as a group** as the beneficiaries of patriarchy. Socialists see **working-class men** as also exploited, and therefore as possible **allies**.

#### iv) Key concepts
Radical: **sexual politics, sex class, the personal is political**. Socialist: **dual systems, family wage, double burden, social reproduction**.

#### v) Methods and campaigns
Radical feminism built **rape crisis centres and shelters** (the first modern shelter opened in London in ==1971==) and campaigned on **bodily autonomy**. Socialist feminism campaigned on **equal pay, childcare and wages for housework**.

#### vi) Main weakness
Radicals are criticised for **ignoring class and race**: **bell hooks** said they spoke for white, middle-class women. Socialists are criticised for **underplaying violence and sexuality**, which cannot be explained by money alone.

#### vii) One example, two readings
Take a Pakistani woman beaten by her husband for asking to keep her wages. A **radical** reading sees **male control** of her body and choices — patriarchy at work in the home. A **socialist** reading adds that her **economic dependence**, low wages and unpaid housework make it hard for her to leave. Both are true; each school sees one side more clearly.

### 7. What they share

#### i) Common ground
Both reject **liberal reformism** as too shallow. Both see the **family** as a site of oppression. Both accept that **the personal is political**. The difference is one of emphasis — **the body and violence** for radicals, **work and money** for socialists.

#### ii) A shared history
Many socialist feminists began as radicals, and many radicals borrowed Marx's language — Firestone called women a "**class**" and spoke of seizing the "**means of reproduction**". The two schools grew from the **same movement** and the same consciousness-raising groups.

### 8. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Radical insight
The **Women's Action Forum**'s campaign against the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) and later laws on **honour killing** (==2016==) and **rape** (==2021==) reflect the radical insight that **violence controls women**.

#### ii) Socialist insight
Most employed Pakistani women work in **agriculture**, much of it **unpaid**; female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25). The struggles of **Lady Health Workers** and the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act** (==2018==) are socialist-feminist in spirit.

### 9. Conclusion
Feminism is one belief with many explanations. **Radical feminism** sees **patriarchy** as the single root and fights it in the family and the body; **socialist feminism** sees **patriarchy and capitalism** as partners and fights them in the family and the economy. For a country like Pakistan, the two are **partners more than rivals**: the first explains the violence women face, the second the poverty that traps them.

## Why this answer scores
- **Both parts** are answered: a definition with two quoted versions, then a point-by-point comparison.
- The introduction gives a **definition**, the **background** and a one-line **thesis** of the difference.
- Differences are set out under **six sub-headings**, not as two separate essays.
- The **compare figure** lets the examiner see the contrast at a glance.
- **What they share** and a **Pakistan angle** raise the answer above a textbook summary.
